With lower perplexity, a language model can be used for various tasks like language translation, text summarization, chatbots, and many other natural language processing tasks with better results.
Therefore, perplexity is a critical metric to evaluate the performance of a language model and plays a crucial role in the development of natural language processing models.
[27/200] Running: How does mixed precision training benefit model training?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Mixed precision is a training technique which combines both 32-bit floating-point and 16-bit floating-point data types for more effective compute utilization. Since the 16-bit floating-point data type typically requires half the number of bits of the 32-bit floating-point, the use of this data type results in memory savings which can increase training data throughput and potentially allow more models to be trained simultaneously with the same hardware. The 16-bit floating-point data type also has the advantage of requiring less memory bandwidth in compute operations, which can be beneficial when using ultra-low latency DRAMs which store large amounts of model data.

When used as a part of a sufficient implementation, mixed precision training can provide a more efficient training solution with improved training throughput at a lower power consumption. Furthermore, combining mixed precision training with other features such as support for adaptive learning rates and gradient accumulation can allow model training that previously was unfeasible to be implemented.

In this example, the training progress of a deep neural network (DNN) model is compared using eight different data types. During this comparison, the DNN model is trained with the same configuration and hardware conditions and the progress is measured based on the number of training iterations per second. Note that this effect is closely related to GPU training throughput and can also be measured using other performance metrics such as the number of training throughputs per second.

Reference: NVIDIA DALI Mixed Precision Example

Training performance takeaway:

Figure 1: Model throughput for different combinations of training data types.

When using mixed precision training, a significant improvement was observed for the number of training iterations per second. Using a combination of TensorFloat-32 (TF32) or float16 and the native 32-bit floating-point data types resulted in 94.4% and 85.5% increases in training throughput respectively, compared to using the 32-bit floating-point data type exclusively. This result was expected as the mixed precision data types provided decreased memory requirements and less dependent memory bandwidth usage that resulted in improved memory bandwidth performance.

The loss of precision when using 16-bit floating-point and TF32 numerical values had no adverse effects on runtime training performance. In fact, the loss of precision can be compensated for to some degree by using adaptive learning rates to ensure that model performance is not impacted.

Summary:

When implementing deep learning model training on GPUs or other hardware, it is beneficial to also consider techniques such as mixed precision training during the design phase. Using mixed precision training can provide several performance improvements when compared to using a single data type, such as reducing memory bandwidth utilization due to decreased memory requirements and result in fewer MACs as well as potentially reducing the number of memory accesses for compute operations.
[28/200] Running: What is the function of the softmax layer in transformers?...

