We discuss each database operator as a contender for differential analysis and identify join as the one that introduces the most error (by multiplying absolute errors). Since expressing information using more than 1 bit always reduces the basis of exponential aggregation, we propose a **speculative decoding mechanism** for joins that targets objects with high description length and uses a database query execution model based on *negative binomial thinning*.

In particular, [*speculative decoding*](https://www.cs.dm.unipi.it/~lucia/pubbl_pdf/fqt16.pdf) is an error reducing approach for query processing in [floating point databases](http://www.cs.utah.edu/~germain/PPS_Files/IEEE1784-2012%20Standard%20for%20Floating-Point%20Arithmetic.pdf): it allows for a **controlled balance between density and precision of information** during query execution. This approach differs from [*error-free floating point processing*](http://cacm.acm.org/magazines/2012/4/146407-error-free-floating-point-processing/fulltext) as a solution to accuracy issues in numerical ranges (i.e., double precision) by using embedded resources in CPUs, such as hardware FMAs. [*Speculative decoding*](https://www.cs.dm.unipi.it/~lucia/pubbl_pdf/fqt16.pdf) does not rely on hardware implementation; rather, it enables exploiting the **imprecision tolerance of the domain** in query engines.

**How does speculative decoding reduce latency?**

Speculative decoding takes advantage of the **imprecision tolerance** of relational databases and enables better control of the query execution model. More specifically, **speculative decoding** allows for an early termination strategy of the processing at any rewrite level, thus dramatically **reducing the number** of necessary iterations in a join and participants involved in the related exchange.

**Example**

Consider a query of the form:

```
