The authors of the above linked paper “FReLU: Residual Blocks with Factorized Factrelu Activation for Realtime Semantic Segmentation” (which you appear to be citing) indicate that they have designed the FReLU block specifically so that spatial dimensionality (width and height) is the same before and after the convolution operation, such that reshaping is unnecessary. Specifically, they use the combination of average pooling (average any pair of patches) followed by nearest neighbor upsampling (which is simply expanding the existing values into a larger 2D tensor of the desired size).

In other words, they define the following standard convolution block:

\[\boxed{\boxed{{\rm\color{red}{\text{Convolution block (}}c_{\text{out}}{\rm\color{red}{)}} \equiv {\rm Input} \rightarrow {\rm Conv} \rightarrow {\rm BN} \rightarrow {\rm Activation} \rightarrow {\rm Output}}}\]

