\[\boxed{\boxed{{\rm\color{red}{\text{FReLU block (}c_{\text{out}}{\rm\color{red}{)}} \equiv {\rm Input} \rightarrow {\rm AP} \rightarrow {\rm Factorized ReLU} \rightarrow {\rm NN\uparrow2} \rightarrow{\rm Pooling boundary} \rightarrow {\rm Sum}}}}\]

where \({\rm AP}\) is average pooling (red dot), \({\rm NN\uparrow2}\) is nearest neighbor upsampling (blue circle) by a factor of 2 in x and y dimensions, and \({\rm Pooling boundary}\) (green triangle) extracts the interior pieces so that tensor dimensions match with Activation and Sum inputs and outputs. Therefore, \({\rm AP}\) and \({\rm NN\uparrow2}\) are paired together to have no effect on the number of feature map pixels, and \({\rm Pooling boundary}\) simply removes the boundary pixels introduced by \({\rm NN\uparrow2}\).

So, here is an example of FReLU block combined with Convolution block operations, to add their branch into the residual path of a 3x3 group of original pixels:

\[\boxed{\boxed{{\rm\color{red}{FReLU+Convolution\ block (}}c_{\text{new}}{\rm\color{red}{)}} \equiv {\rm Input} \rightarrow {\rm AP} \rightarrow {\rm Factorized ReLU} \rightarrow {\rm NN\uparrow2} \rightarrow\overbrace{\underbrace{{\rm Pooling\ boundary}}_{\text{shape}\mathbf{((W,H)}}^{\text{size}\frac{(W-3)}{2},\frac{(H-3)}{2})}{\rm\rightarrow {\rm Convolution\ block (}}c_{\text{new}}{\rm)}\rightarrow{\rm+\ñosigned{\frac{{1}}{{2}}\text{ (}}c_{\text{new}}{\rm)}}\rightarrow {\rm Output}}}\]

In the standard ResNet block, the convolution takes place after the main branch and the skip branch are added, and the activation (ReLU) comes afterward in one of the stages. But in this alternative form of block, composite FReLU+Con
[92/200] Running: Describe a pipeline for deploying LLaMA 2 in production....

🧠 LLM Response:


