🧠 LLM Response:

Triton Inference Server is an open-source, scalable, multi-GPU model serving system. It was designed to support deploying deep learning models from TensorFlow, TensorRT, PyTorch, ONNX, and others. It’s built by NVIDIA and comes with great support for GPU deployment.

What is the benefit for deploying our TensorFlow models to Triton Inference Server?
When compared with other serving systems that support GPUs, TRITON Inference Server is an absolute winner, currently offering multiple deployment options (REST, gRPC, and Kubernetes), as well as the ability to use multi-GPU deployment (even with mixed-precision for faster inference). TensorFlow alone does offer TensorFlow Serving based on gRPC. As TRITON Inference Server offers additional functionality, it’s worth considering.

What is required for deploying TensorFlow models to TRITON Inference Server?
To deploy TensorFlow models to TRITON Inference Server, there is no requirement for any modification to your existing TensorFlow models. The only requirement is to export the model with the TensorFlow Ops API. When you have the exported model using saved_model in SavedModel format, presumably within a directory saved_model/1, you can now create a configuration file depending on the kind of server launch you want (local, REST…), and launch the server as described in the official guide.

All arguments according the guide that are required for the configuration file cmd_param are:

> — model-repository=path/to/triton_directory
>
>
> Set the directory where models will be loaded and saved. It should be the directory where the saved_model is exported.
>
>
>
> — backend=tensorflow
>
>
> Specifies that you are going to serve TensorFlow models.
>
>
>
> — model-control-mode=explicit
>
>
> The models should be loaded explicitly. It’s worth mentioning that from version 1.8.0, default value is AUTOMATIC. It means that listing models in model-repository/models.txt is no longer required. However, for hands-on tests, it’s worth setting up explicitly to understand the layout.

Additionally, if you’ve developed the model locally, the last two arguments should be added:

> — model-name=model_TEST
>
>
> Following instructions and serving the model saved_model/1, the model name should be model_TEST.
>
>
>
> — model-version=1
>
>
> Depending on your saved_model directories, in case there is only one directory it could be model_TEST/1 or just 1 if there is only one model.

