[108/200] Running: What are residual connections in transformers?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Residual connections are an important component of transformer models that can contribute to their high performance. In transformer models, each sub-layer has a feedforward layer followed by a residual connection (which adds the initial input of the sub-layer to the output of the feedforward layer) and a layer normalization layer. The residual connections help the transformer learn long-range dependencies and enable faster training and better convergence by reducing the vanishing gradient problem. They also make the transformer architecture deeper and more complex, allowing it to handle more complex tasks and data. Residual connections have been shown to improve the performance of transformer models in machine translation, language modeling, and other natural language processing tasks.
[109/200] Running: How to handle long sequences in generation?...

🧠 LLM Response:

===================================

Handling very long sequences with deep learning approaches may be a bottleneck for the following reasons:

- **Computational expense**: longer sequences are often associated with substantial computational expense in terms of GPU/TPU memory needs, computation time.
- **Intrinsic limitations of the architecture**: vanilla transformer encoder-decoder with or without the attention layers may lose sequential aspect of information if the sequence length increases (longer sequences will imply hundreds/thousands of computationally expensive and complex layers).
- **Limitation of the training data**: in case of embodied conversational agents, human-human interaction studies may have a limited amount of sequential data (even if we have a large dataset there will be only 4 or 5 conversations with possibly a low number of statements per conversation).

Possible solutions (but not limited to):

- **Chunking**: Chunking simulates a pointer moving along a sequence. It allows handling longer sequences by breaking them into shorter segments of, for example 32 timesteps, and passing them to the model, one after the other.
- **Windowing**: Windowing chunks up an entire sequence into smaller independent and overlapping sub-sequences of, for example, 32 timesteps. This way of slicing up sequences allows to the model to capture long-term dependencies by sliding the window/chunk across the sequence.
- **Sliding Window Variant (see image below)**: Hybrid semi-natural solution. By updating the contextual window size, the model is capable to learn median (**K**)" range term dependencies (long and short range) and sequential dependencies.

![Sliding Window Variant](../Images/SlidingWindowConcept.png)

Note that these approaches are often compatible. For instance in the above image, the hyperbolic shape was produced using a sequence of chunking with varying chunk size (1000-150-1000 words) which ensures both longer and shorter sequential information.

Please note adding complexity to the model training generally leads to degradation in model performance, however these techniques are not mutually inclusive and can be combined to ensure performance/control trade-offs based on desired characteristics.

How to impose boundaries or rules with generation
====================================

Adding additional constraints to the model:

- **Speculating towards the end of the sequences**. From a 3D image to text generation application perspective, generating sensory observations beyond the actual world limits may result in descriptive bias.
	+ *Solution* : Introduce a hyper-parameter for terminating the sequence, e.g. by using a TF-Keras lambda-callback along with an ending token --> [Transformer Agent Example](https://github.com/LaurensAarnoudse/Transformer_Agent)
- **Human-imposed bias on the generated output** in cases where the pre-processing step may not be capable to handle exceptional situations.
	+ *Solution* : Design a TF-Keras custom callback for constraining the generator output if needed, and/or at creation define custom classes that inherit from the [**CallBack**](https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/keras/callbacks/CallBack) and inject the conditions within the constructor.
		- e.g. [EpisodicEOSCallBack Example](https://github.com/agLocalAI/ACIC/blob/main/handbook/ConceptualFramework/notebooks/FlashGPT-Episodic.ipynb)
- **Natural Language Commonsense Validation (NLCV)**. Verifying the common sense associated with strongly entailing inference steps using factual knowledge during the transformer forward/decoder pass.
	+ *Solution* : Implement the NLCV with pre/post-processing, namely enclose decoding or generation as a standalone pipeline.
- **Natural Language Disambiguation Validation (NLDV)**.  During generation effective NLDV requires processing encoder information into topics which can be handled within the pre-processing step.
	+ *Solution* : During preprocessing armed with the pre-computed topics, sub-topics and models, the TF-Keras **_predict___** function can be used to select the correct topic, sub-topic or otherwise re-train using new/correct data.
- **Supporting NLP without Linguistic Success** e.g. Successful Semantic Construction of sentences during generation even when the NLP results in a non-
[110/200] Running: Explain the use of flash attention v2....

🧠 LLM Response:
 Unlike flash
attention v1 flash attention v2 does not use a learnable
QkV matrix to project Q, K, and V to the attention space and
instead linearly combines the representations from lower
layers. How does this differ from feedforward networks?

Flash Attention v1 projects the Q, K, and V matrices to
the attention space using a learnable QkV matrix. In contrast,
Flash Attention v2 does not use a learnable QkV matrix but
instead linearly combines the representations from lower
layers to get Q, K, and V matrices. This means that in Flash
Attention v2, the Q, K, and V matrices are computed based on
the lower-level representations, while in Flash Attention v1,
the Q, K, and V matrices are learned independently of the
input sequence.

Feedforward networks, on the other hand, are a type of neural
network layer that applies a linear transformation followed by
a non-linear activation function to the input. Unlike attention
mechanisms, feedforward networks do not explicitly compute
queries, keys, and values or perform a weighted sum based on
the similarity between queries and keys. Instead, they apply a
simple linear transformation to the input, followed by a non-linear
activation function to introduce non-linearity and increase the
representational capacity of the network.

In summary, Flash Attention v2 differs from feedforward networks
in that it explicitly computes queries, keys, and values based on
lower-level representations, and performs a weighted sum based on
the similarity between queries and keys, whereas feedforward networks
apply a simple linear transformation to the input and introduce
non-linearity through a non-linear activation function.
[111/200] Running: What is a greedy search in inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Search — Find the optimal solution (with respect to some criteria) or the best possible approximate solution, because finding the optimal solution is intractable.

Greedy search — In greedy search, in every state, the best action is taken, where the action is selected towards local optimum, not the global optimum. This approach can get stuck at a state that is not the optimal, but it works satisfactorily well in many real-life situations. Greedy search has exponential worst-case performance, and polynomial average-case performance.

Example: For a Salesman problem, consider a set of n cities (or nodes), and the salesman’s job is to visit every city (node) only once, but return to the starting location. Greedy search would be looking for the nearest unvisited city (node), instead of looking for the optimal path.

In 1957, George Dantzig coined the term Algorithm to describe these types of problems.

If you want to learn more, visit Greedy Search Algorithm .

Thanks,
Pradeep
