🧠 LLM Response:

---------------------------------------------------------

**Speculative decoding** is a term which came into existence with the introduction
of **automatic speech recognition (ASR)** by Google in their
[Google Docs](https://www.blog.google/products/docs/hit-return-voice-typing-google-docs)
text-editor. Following the release of its proprietary implementation, wide
interest from academic and research communities appeared. While the underlying
mechanisms may differ, the main concept of speculative decoding involves predicting
continuously, by targeting user intent (the user’s intended text) and downwardly
applying language models towards texts that predicate this intent during the voice
input from an user, essentially by **predicting what the user meant**.

<p align="center">
  <img align="center" src="./assets/docs.png" width="400" />
</p>

From a personal point, I've had the luck of being involved in different ASR/NLU teams at Google in Zurich and London. I've seen the research progression and the introduction of other models following the Neural Architectures for Transformation (NAT) family, that helped on different tasks.

Contrary to what is implemented on Google Docs, speculative decoding is not implemented on Google's search. The user intention for Google search is to look for results that match their query, thus making the use of such decoding irrelevant.

Some of my colleagues at Google have implemented **speculative autocomplete**, making the experience of using a keyboard on a smartphone more responsive. This is an ongoing improvement within Android Cloud Research team.

<p align="center">
  <img align="center" src="./assets/autocomplete.png" width="400" />
</p>

A bit of history of speculative decoding
----------------------------------------

Less known to the general public, this technology was already in some forms in MS Word 2010 for 10 years prior Google Docs’ breakthrough implementation, although in different terms and technological paradigm, with a more basic version of this technology.

Speculative decoding is an upgrade on **parallel typing models** which can be seen as linear decoding that offers sentence completion snippets to the user during inference.

The linear decoding implemented in Google Keyboard really is the same implemented in earlier versions of Google Translate, although applied differently. The key difference with **speculative decoding** is incorporating user intent biases while applying the *logits*, which allows the model to be more responsive towards a user’s expectation.

<p align="center">
  <img align="center" src="./assets/lineal.jpg" width="600" />
</p>

The baseline used was a linear model that predicts each embedding, generates it and then predicts the next one until the end of a sentence is reached. While the model lasts a few seconds to predict an entire text, the practical accompaniment of a user’s utterance is possible. This contrasts with the latency shown on text-prediction methods a few years ago, which showed a terrible range for the user experience.

