Thanks Alex! How accurate the inter-token latency metric is though in measuring the performance of a language model? I ask this because depending on how efficient (or inefficient) our code is, the latency of a request can vary widely.

As I understand it, inter-token latency is the average time taken to generate a token, which in turn is computed by calculating the difference in time between the beginning of the request and the time taken to generate a token in the same request. But if we have a long running request, and along with the model, there are also other processes running in the background taking up CPU & memory, would inter-token latency accurately measure the model's performance? And how do we avoid the pitfalls, if any, associated with inter-token latency is not a good metric?

Another possible method I can think of is to measure the time taken to generate all tokens within a request and divide that by the number of tokens. This would give us a more wholesome latency metric, right?

Hi @user15570315, thanks for your questions!

In general, inter-token latency is the best way to assess the true responsiveness of a model as it reflects the dynamic and "thought" processes of a generation task in real-time. However, the surrounding system and environmental factors will inevitably influence both online and offline performance measurements, so the key is always going to be controlling for any extraneous factors that may skew the results.

I'd approach your considerations in the following order:

1. Ensuring that the system and surrounding codebase are optimized for performance (and to the extent possible, eliminating any unnecessary competing processes if you have control over these resources).
2. Adopting established industry best practices for performance benchmarking and monitoring, in particular, **controlled experiments** using measuring points throughout the entire systems and the platforms for which your application is being built and ultimately deployed.

In terms of the second question about the adequacy of your alternative proposal; I would caution against simply taking the sum of the individual token times to compute a latency figure since this would not account for many _non-uniform delays _such as latency jitter that may occur following execution of the initialLM call on the server.

Essentially, inter-token latency is more than merely a  measure of wall-clock time, it can also capture the other mechanisms associated with the model's internal response behavior reflective of real-world user experience, including:

1. The speed of pre-processing methods (e.g., tokenizer) invoked by the underlying API.
2. System load2 & waiting times for asynchronous tasks to complete when waiting for think-time.
3. I/O and disk scheduling delays during data exchange with server.
4. Interrupt handling or any other overhead that may occur.

And these mechanisms are not always uniform in size.

One thing you can do to ensure you are optimizing and monitoring the true cost of each token is to learn about low-level tools such as perf and strace to help track and debug processes inside any supporting codebase and  further, to ensure your monitors are triggered by specific events in the codebase to capture the true processing latency = the sum of processing time + delays (interrupt latency, queueing time, etc.)

As an example, interrupted latency is usually never measured since it cannot be guaranteed what the device/kernel is doing when a blocking process (your LM service) unblocks. But using tracing tools can help you optimize your real-time latencies during these conditions, so that the delays are not excluded from any performance monitoring or metrics used to assess the application's user experience.

Thanks, Alex

