[136/200] Running: How does `max_new_tokens` differ from `max_length`?...

🧠 LLM Response:


The `max_new_tokens` parameter is used to set the maximum number of new tokens to generate in a text completion request. It controls the length of the generated response by only considering the number of new tokens to add to the prompt.

On the other hand, `max_length` is a parameter used to set the maximum total length of the input and generated text combined. It takes into account both the prompt and the generated response, and if the sum of their lengths exceeds the specified `max_length`, the generated text will be truncated to fit within the limit.

In summary, `max_new_tokens` controls the length of the generated text based on the number of new tokens, while `max_length` sets an upper limit for the total length of the input and generated text.
[137/200] Running: Explain the difference between loss and perplexity....

🧠 LLM Response:
 Then explain in your own words which one is better to use while training a model and why.

Loss and perplexity are both measures of how well a model is performing. However, they are not the same thing.

Loss is a scalar value that measures the difference between the predicted output of a model and the actual output. The goal of training a machine learning model is to minimize this difference, i.e., to find the model parameters that result in the lowest possible loss.

Perplexity, on the other hand, is a measure of how well a probability model predicts a sample. It is calculated as 2 to the power of the cross-entropy loss, which is the negative log likelihood of the actual output given the predicted output. A lower perplexity score indicates that the model is better at predicting the actual output.

Between the two, perplexity is a better measure to use while training a model, because it takes into account the uncertainty of the predicted output. A model that always predicts the same output, regardless of the input, would have a low loss but a high perplexity. On the other hand, a model that is able to accurately predict the output with high confidence would have a low perplexity. Therefore, using perplexity as a measure of performance during training encourages the model to be more confident in its predictions, which is generally desirable.
