[158/200] Running: What is a quantized model and how is it served?...

🧠 LLM Response:


A quantized model refers to a deep learning model with optimized parameters that have been quantized. Quantization reduces the model size and inference latency by reducing the precision of the models. Not all models, especially the relatively small ones, require quantization. Generally, models with hundreds of megabytes or more are considered large and are usually served post quantization.

Quantization differs from pruning, where all the parameters of a model are not optimized but only a fraction of them are pruned. Like quantization, pruning reduces inference latency (upto 5x) but generally has a relatively smaller impact on reducing model size (upto 2x).

Quantization: 1 bit integer quantization in aws deepracer3 results in to 3x reductions in both inference time and model size, 7.4x reduction in bandwidth and minimal loss in accuracy. https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/experimenting-with-quantization-for-the-aws-deepracer-reinforcement-learning-model-on-aws-lambda-at-the-edge-for-faster-inference/

Pruning: 65% sparse model(65% only zero parameters in model) with 225% higher inference speed, 30% smaller model size, less memory usage and lower power consumption. More than 200x less memory on edge device. https://towardsdatascience.com/learn-how-pruning-can-help-you-reduce-your-models-size-and-increase-its-inference-speed-5000cd679504

There are different ways to serve a quantized model. The most used server are as follows:

* Tensorflow : Tensorflow model server
* Pytorch : Pytorch Serving
* Chainer : ChainerMN
* Caffe2 : Caffe2 Serve

There are some other servers, such as MXNet Model Server and Clipper, that can serve different frameworks.

To share a quantized model for distributed inference there are several open-source serving frameworks like Tensorflow Model Server, Clipper, and TorchServe.

What are quantized low precision weights?

Quantization refers to the rules that the floating point values of the model's weights are mapped to at inference time and stored as integers in memory. In Quantized low precision weights that rule is mapping floating point weights to 8-bit integers or less. There are various methods to perform this quantization such as uniform, logarithmic, power-of-two, etc. The most common method is uniform quantization, which reduces the dynamic range of the floating point numbers to uniform partitions (bins) and then maps them to integers.

Benefits of Quantization

Quantization offers several benefits for inference, such as:

* Memory footprint reduction: The model parameters are represented in a lower bit width representation, e.g. 8-bits, so the memory storage required is reduced. This is especially important in embedded devices and edge inference.
* Performance improvement: Accessing lower precision integer data allows for a simplification of the algorithms used in the hardware architecture of the CPUs, GPUs and TPUs, e.g. Fused Multiply-and-Add operations, and enables a faster computation of the matrix operations used in the DNN models.
* Power reduction: Lower precision integer data accessing operates at lower voltage to consume less energy.

Is fine-tuning needed for quantized models?

Fine-tuning is usually not required for quantized models, at least in a large-scale production environment. However, several approaches perform fine-tuning after quantization to optimize the model's performance.

Instance floats data type and 8bit weights have 1-3% loss in accuracy, post-training quantization with some fine-tuning is recommended for this setup. Tensorflow's default quantizer is post-training quantization with symmetric uniform quantization of activations and weights, and add some fine-tuning.

How is quantization done?

Quantization is done in two ways :-

* Post-training quantization
* Quantized training

Post-training Quantization

Post-training Quantization is the process of quantizing the trained full precision model. It generally applies when a large enough set is available to estimate the distribution of activations at inference time.

