🧠 LLM Response:

----------------------------------------

What you want to do, is stream one token JSON object at a time from a stream of JSON objects using cURL. This is
an example of using jq (the JSON parser for the shell) to process JSON tokens.

	curl -s https://example.com/api/v1/inbox | jq -n --stream '[inputs | { _type: "MyType", message: . }}] | animals[]'

This will stream all items of type 'MyType' from the inbox stream, and then stream one JSON object of type `MyType`
at a time.

How to stream JSON tokens from a local file?
-------------------------------------------

Alternatively, if you just want to stream tokens from a local file, you can still do it using jq.

Here's an example:

	jq -n --stream --file test.json '[inputs | { _type: "MyType", message: . }}] | animals[]'

This will stream one JSON object of type `MyType` at a time, created from the tokens in the `test.json` file.

For further details, consult the jq documentation [1](https://stedolan.github.io/jq/manual/) and the `--stream`
section in particular [2](https://stedolan.github.io/jq/manual/v1.5/#--streamoutput:(stream|{}|.[])).
For additional examples and ways to play with this, visit jqplay [3](http://jqplay.org/).

 Addendum:

Once you have mastered how to use the --stream option, using the --stream-encoding option can allow you to
 print UTF-8 JsonText.

For additional ways to process streams using pipelines of Unix shell commands, read https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/Output-redirecting.html#Output-redirecting. It's awesome for breaking down streams of data.

[1]: https://stedolan.github.io/jq/manual/
[2]: https://stedolan.github.io/jq/manual/v1.5/#--streamoutput:(stream|{}|.[]))
