[164/200] Running: What are server cold starts in model serving?...

🧠 LLM Response:


In model serving there are two fundamental operations: model inferencing and model loading. Model inference is the operation of actually evaluating the model on inputs to generate the required outputs, while model loading is the operation of instantiating the model in memory and loading precomputed model artifacts such as parameter values. There are broadly two kinds of serving deployments:

> 1. Online predictive serving platforms use serving through long lived server-side applications. Inference is performed on demand for incoming requests. Such deployments reuse preloaded models from one request to another. Thus the amortized latency of loading the model is very low.
>
> 2. Offline serving of trained models use model serving for serving batch prediction requests and post processing pipelines. Such systems typically load a model for processing a single batch request and then throw away the model.

Model loading for offline services is called cold model loading as models are loaded from scratch for each processing and the overheads involved are much more substantial and critical towards the overall serving cost. A cold model serving request consists of two phases:

> 1. Cold Model Load Time: Cold Model Load Time corresponds to model loading cost. This is the amount of time required to load the model to process a single request and is measured as the percentage(or time) of cold model loads per requests
>
> 2. Time-to-Predict: Time-to-Predict corresponds to the model inferencing cost. Once model is loaded it takes time to produce signle inference.

Model Serving Systems focus on keeping the overall request latency low, which means we need to optimize both the First Byte or response time(concatenating (1) and (2) in the figure above) while providing capability to serve large pool of users. The Cold Model Load Time is typically delegated to the underlying frameworks and libraries like TensorFlow Serving, ONNX Runtime, Clipper etc. Each of these frameworks have varied approaches for Cold Model Load Time optimization:

> 1. Application Level Caching: Frameworks like TensorFlow Serving provides the capability to reduce the cold model load time through caching. Other frameworks like NVIDIA TensorRT offers python API for loading model to caching the models locally at application level to provide faster server start up time. 
>
> 2. Container Level Caching: Frameworks like Apache TVM provide container level model caching by using Singularity and Docker container to improve the cold model load time at cost of underlying compute roots like Ubuntu, UbuntuServer or even Linux root filesystem
>
> 3. Shared Library Based Optimizations: Some frameworks like ONNX Runtime have decided to optimize the cold model load time and distributive down the shared libraries. We can see the shared libraries of such frameworks having numerous pre-compiled DLL binaries for the commonly used ML operations.

While these methods of optimizations are slightly different we will see how they have managed reduce the cold model load time for model serving systems. They are all well known across the industry, for example the famous example of TensorFlow Serving providing the capability to built and serve the ML models (Wheels) on top of custom built Dockerfiles through Docker Hub and BentoBox. This approach was envisioned to reduce the overhead involved in building custom models which avoids frozen TensorFlow graph recompilation/optimization step. We will see how implementations of these frameworks (whether in terms of shivering models, caching the models in application level, containers or shared library based optimizations) have presented a ratio of Cold Model Load time for a single model to the time required to serve a batch of 10 models with time-to-predict the same across the board.

In order to understand the impact of these optimizations on poor man's serving scenarios, we took up a simple TFlite based ML model based representative use-case and used all the above methods (whether TensorFlow Serving, ONNX Runtime, and NVIDIA TensorRT or even Apache TVM) to reap the value. We will see how these optimizations play a critical role in overall cost(Both computational and memory) in model serving.

There are multiple parameters in each of these frameworks for optimization. We indulged onto creating various operator fusions from the allowed low level operations by the operators using each of these frameworks(TFlite, ONNX Runtime and NVIDIA TensorRT) followed by XLA compiled servings and refer to them as Cold Fusions.

As demonstrated above we presented the ratio of the Cold Model Load Time with respect to the Time-to-Pred
[165/200] Running: Explain how to avoid padding inefficiencies....

🧠 LLM Response:


Padding inefficiencies occur when processing a large number of small blocks instead of a small number of large blocks.

To avoid padding inefficiencies, you can:

1. Increase the size of the blocks being processed. This reduces the number of blocks and minimizes the amount of padding required.
2. Use a block size that is a multiple of the input size, so that no padding is needed.
3. Use a block cipher mode that does not require padding, such as Cipher Feedback (CFB) or Output Feedback (OFB).
4. Use a stream cipher instead of a block cipher. Stream ciphers do not require padding because they encrypt data one byte at a time.
5. Use a padding scheme that minimizes the amount of padding required, such as zero padding or PKCS#7 padding. Zero padding adds a single zero byte at the end of the plaintext, while PKCS#7 padding adds a number of bytes equal to the block size minus the length of the plaintext.
6. Implement a padding removal algorithm in the decryption process to remove any excess padding added during encryption.
7. Use techniques like compression or tokenization to reduce the number of small blocks into larger ones, thereby reducing the amount of padding required.
8. Implement a caching mechanism to reuse previously processed blocks and reduce the number of new blocks required.

By implementing these measures, you can reduce or eliminate padding inefficiencies, thereby improving the performance of your encryption and decryption processes.
