[167/200] Running: How to track per-prompt latency in logs?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Use `$SLOTX` and friends to add metadata to the prompt. Then use this in your program to tag relevant logs.

(This is a sequel to [How to track]\(logs-for-service-level-objectives\) Service Level Objectives. It covers a similar topic—using metadata in your logs for better analysis, but this time focussed at prompt level.)

A lot of my work these days involves custom SQL `SELECT` statements run multiple times with different variables for small changes in output. For example, each time I need to output a different `year` part of a `DATE` or `DATETIME` column.

This type of query can quickly lead to million of queries for a small increase in pageviews as the number of permutations increases with the number of variables. Your SLO objectives will quickly be destryed as your program races to run and support all possible variables.

This post will cover **how to track individual prompt latency in logs** to help you spot days where prompts become slower than normal, helping you quickly diagnose and fix problems.

## Overview

Briefly, we can extract a small injection into our prompt template so that all prompt text is prepended with metadata, such as `[YYYY]`. For example:

```sql
$SLOTX [2018] SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users
WHERE year=2018
```

Another example could be multiple metadata items:

```sql
$SLOTX [YYYY][DEFAULT SELECT] SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users
WHERE year=$SLOTX[YYYY] AND order_status=$SLOTX[DEFAULT SELECT]
```

In our logs, each line could look a little like:

```
[2018] [DEFAULT SELECT] "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE year=2018 AND order_status=paid" 0.03
[2019] [DEFAULT SELECT] "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE year=2019 AND order_status=paid" 10.00
[2018] [DEFAULT INSERT] "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE year=2018 AND order_status=draft" 0.15
[2018] [DEFAULT SELECT] "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users WHERE year=2018 AND order_status=draft" 2.50
```


## Extracting the metadata of a prompt

The first step is extracting the metadata. You can do this using the [`rprompt`][extract] module, described below.

Firstly, we should define the SLO we are tracking. In this case, I'm tracking `[]` (Start of line brackets), since it's easy to extract and allows me to override any other custom text extracted by developers in future.

```js
// promot.js
const rprompt = require('catalog/rprompt');

const promptScope = rprompt.forValid(
    // We only apply if the start of line is detected
    // This will override any existing text at the start of line
    (line) => line.indexOf('[]') === 0,

    // Then extract the data
    (line) => {
        // Extract year before the first space
        const year = line.replace(/^\W*(\d{4})\W.*/, '$1');

        // Extract the default_select (everything else separated
        // by closing and opening braces)
        const select = line.replace(
            /\[(?:\W*\w+)*\]\s+(\[.*?\]\s+)?"(.*?)"/s, '$1'
        );

        return { year, select };
    }
);

module.exports = {
    '[]': promptScope,
    // Override any metadata from our developers
    '[': promptScope,
}

```

We can now `require` this file in our main program and include the SLO into the leaderboard based on the extracted year.

```js
// leaderboard.js
const rprompt = require('utils/prompt').forValid(
    // Strip whitespace before and after the braces
    /\s*\[(?:\w+\s+)*\]\s+
[168/200] Running: Explain streaming benchmarks with high concurrency....

🧠 LLM Response:
 How is streaming benchmark different from traditional RDBMS performance benchmarking?

Streaming throughput performance benchmarking with high concurrency focus on testing how well the software (streaming engine) can ingress/egress multiple data stream connections concurrently where each data stream connection may leverage a single or multiple processing threads. Optimizing an engine for streaming throughput performance is different than doing so for traditional RDBMS performance because streaming engines typically do not have the luxury of exactly knowing which records in a data stream will be required for a given query.

When we benchmark a relational database such as Postgres in high concurrency mode, we expectively measure query latency and throughput for given concurrency level. We will do it under write-only, read-only, and mixed workloads, as we want to measure the engine's performance with all of these workloads. On the other hand, when benchmarking high concurrency in a streaming engine, it is not necessary to have separate workloads. Well-defined benchmark metric with high concurrency settings should be the focus here. Closely related to this concept is the "burstiness" in workloads. RDBMs require steady and predictable workloads, where streaming engines perform better with more burstiness in workloads.

To achieve high concurrency in streaming, a throughput-optimized engine will use strategies such as:

1. Multi-threaded ingress/egress (buffers) and parse/compress (CPU) operations.
2. Sharded task queue design for processing jobs of each connection.
3. Data partitioning for distributed processing.
4. Non-blocking I/O (network and disk).

Many modern data processing engines make use of Kafka or similar publish/subscribe-based messaging systems to build a streaming pipeline. In these environments, benchmarking consideration will extend to topics and partitions design, and ensuring such settings match with the use-case workload (number of consumers).

Moreover, streaming engines are often integrated into elastic environments that allow them to scale up/down with workload demand. In this context, benchmarking for horizontal scalability and self-healing would be vital performance metrics to consider.
