🧠 LLM Response:


## Overview

Symbols, punctuations and emojis are handled well by most tokenizers, including the ones implemented by Google, NLTK, SpaCy and CoNLL. However, the handling of symbols and punctuations will be slightly different depending on the tokenizer settings one uses.

There are also few methods that can be used to tweak the tokenizer behavior that I've listed down at the end of the article.

In this article, I am going to cover the tokenization of symbols and punctuations in the following place:

- tokenizer.whitespace
- tokenizer.space
- tokenizer.punkt
- tokenizer.mw

Each of the tokenizer has its own set of attributes and methods, and I will go through the URL used later to list out the following properties, attributes and methods. In general, the behavior of symbols and punctuations can be overridden by enabling or disabling those settings, as well as with the use of the methods provided.

In the following sections, I will go through the punctuations and emoji handling of the tokenizers for the respective languages as well as answers the questions for the specific problems.

**All the tokenization in the following sections is done by leaving out only the options for the text that I want to understand, and nothing else. Hence, the tokenization may not illustrate the full functionality of the tokenizer.**

## Ground Truth

As I will be comparing the tokenizer outputs with the ground truth at the end, it is important to understand the true segmentation of the text without using the tokenizer.

The ground truth follows these rules (`>` indicates a line break, but is excluded in the ground truth):
- a consecutive space will split the text into a separate token
- a symbol indicates a complete token and the text after the symbol is considered as a new token that does not contain the puctuations
- emoji is a separate token

The following is the ground truth for the example emoji and text that I will be using in this article:

(Note: when it comes to character readability with the Unicode (HEX) for symbols and emojis below, I tend to see the multi-character string as a single character. I will try to contrast the mult-charater string to its readability here as the ground truth the tokenizer will try to follow.)

```
1️⃣ Hi, I'm Austin!
>1: 1️⃣ <2> Hi <3> , <4> I <5> ' <6> m <7> Austin <8> ! <9>

2️⃣ Austin.is.a.good.guy
>2: 2️⃣ <2> Austin <3> . <4> is <5> . <6> a <7> . <8> good <9> . <10> guy <11>
```

## Tokenizer - Space (tokenizer.space)

The following is a simple example on how the space tokenizer parses out the emoji and the text.

```
from nltk.tokenize import SpaceTokenizer
tokens = SpaceTokenizer().tokenize("1️⃣ Hi, I'm Austin!")
```

This would result in the following:

```
1️⃣ Hi, I'm Austin!
>['1️⃣', '', 'Hi,', '', 'I', "'", 'm', 'Austin', '!']
```

**Conclusion:**

Generally speaking, the SpaceTokenizer is designed to split up strings based on the whitespace characters.

This iss something that the Space tokenizer is less meant for the following goal:
- Split up a string including all punctuations
- Split up a string so that every token is something that has semantic meaning

Not with the simple default settings.

Lesson. 

**Always make sure that you check the essential documentation for the tokenizer than using the first apt one you find.**

