## Model size

My experiment goes like this. I created three nets from scratch with the structures in the table below

The building block of these networks is vgg16, which is the network that came out from Oxford VGG Group (http://www.robots.ox.ac.uk/~vgg/research/very_deep), later went online on GitHub (https://github.com/machrisaa/tensorflow-vgg16), that won places in Imagenet many years ago. VGG-16 is the first CNN going to very deep.

I benchmarked the networks on 1080 GPU. The table below shows the results and the comparison to the online model zoo that TensorFlow puts.

The columns in the tables are:

- Number of network layers
- The number of parameters
- The number of FLOPs (1E8) (1E+8)
- The speed in second from a single batch of image (128 pixels)
- The latency in milliseconds

The results indicate that the inference speed is heavily affected by the model size or the number of parameters. It also depends on FLOPS to a great extent.

My interpretation goes with a incremental set-up of the network, which has also been adopted by many other places (like Microsoft, Nvidia, etc.) in their research and product development. Incremental set-up includes some mapping, compression and adjustment based on interpretation, which may lag between model structuring and deployment at the software layer. On the other hand, FLOPS should largely determine the computing power requirement if there is a hardware, then I think latency has to stay the same as FLOPS difference becomes minimum. This seems to be the case, and the constant latency means better quality of service for the GPUSerializer.
