```

But if some `Frame` has some invalid UTF-8 bytes, it will cause panic, so it need to handle decoding errors, return a Custom `decoded_result` which include a `Packet` and a two `bool` variables like:

- `decoding_success`
- `validation_success`

How can I achieve this with `Rust`?

You can use the `Result` type to represent the outcome of the decoding process, giving you information about the success or failure of the decoding as well as any error message. Since your type contains a `Vec`, you might also want to use `iter().collect()` to collect it to a `Result` directly, which would allow you to return an error early on if any byte cannot be decoded.

The `try!` macro from the error-chain crate is quite helpful for this, as it converts `Result` into `!` which is an error type in rust and will panic if result is Error.

You could change your decode method like this:

```
use error_chain::error_chain;

error_chain! {
    foreign_links {
        Io(std::io::Error);
    }
}

struct Packet {
    data: Vec<u8>,
}

impl Packet {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Packet {
            data: vec![],
        }
    }

    fn decode(frame: &Frame) -> Result<Self, Error> {
        let content = frame.content;

        let mut packet = Self::new();
        for c in content {
            match c {
                Ok(byte) => packet.data.push(byte.0),
                Err(err) => return Err(err.into()),
            }
        }

        Ok(packet)
    }
}
