[178/200] Running: What are temperature vs randomness in sampling?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 How does one create a random sample at a specific temperature?

I have seen these terms being used in the context of metropolis hastings algorithms but I am not able to make a specific connection to randomness vs temperature.

Thanks for your patience.

Temperature `T` can be (but is not always) associated with the randomness of sampling in simulated annealing/Metropolis–Hastings. Then, if the temperature is low, the changes in the system are "slight", in the sense that the acceptance probability $$alpha = mining{min}(1, exp((E_{old}-E_{new})/T)),$$ is low, so the likelihood to be accepted is low. At higher temperatures, the changes can be more more random and significantly different from the old state.

From the analogical viewpoint, heating the system up correspondingly lowers its energy barrier, so waves and particles can go over it (in the sense of jumping from one state to another). At lower temperatures, this barrier is higher, so only particles having enough energy can jump high enough to cross the energy barrier.

Samples at specific temperatures can be created by drawing values from a Boltzmann distribution. Although i never did this "by hand", i saw a python code which produced temperature-dependent samples for you. This can be created using the inverse temperature $\beta = 1/T$ defined.

### Temperature in MCMC

Yes, temperature plays a role in markov chains monte carlo and simulated annealing methods. It usually determines the width of the jump distribution which is used in the acceptance criterion. This can be, e.g., the normal distribution in the Metropolis method:
$$
beginitp{array}{rcl}
u &sim& unif(0, 1) ,
v &sim& N(0, T) ,
s_{t+1} &= s_t + v &quad text{if } u < eta(s_t, s_{t+1}) ,
enditp{array}
$$
where $\eta$ is an acceptance probability, e.g.,
$$
eta(s_t, s_{t+1}) = min(1, exp(L(s_{t+1}) - L(s_t))) .
$$
Temperature $T$ or inverse temperature $\beta = 1/T$ can be also used in the happy-valley distribution for accept/reject criterion through $\beta$:
$$
alpha = eta(x, y) = left{beginarray}{ll}
exp( -\beta (y – x) ) & text{ if } x < y < 0 \
1                     & text{ if } 0 le y le x \
exp( \beta (y – x) ) & text{ otherwise }
end{array}\ right.
$$
If $\beta = 0$ (which is equivalent to $T = +infty$), the acceptance rate is $1$, and any proposal gets accepted, leading to an independent sampling. As $\beta$ or $1/T$ grows, the acceptance gets more and more restrictive, and the markov chain starts to resemble random walk. See, for example, page 124 in D. Finkelstein, Mathematical modeling in genetics, Statistical snake oil or mathematical gold?, Comput Math Methods Med, 2013.

The temperature corresponding to the target distribution, e.g., posterior distribution, comes from the bayesian statistical framework. At high temperature, annealing can lead to global optimization which does not depend on the particular data in hand. After reaching a low temperature, annealing with the actual data leads to parameter estimates. The idea is called simulated annealing.

### Temperature and randomness. Housekeeping

In the context of Metropolis–Hastings method, temperature is associated with the randomness. At lower temperatures, changes in the system are slight, in other words, transitions in the generated markov chain are that more similar. At high temperatures, changes in the system are more random and transitions in the markov chain can be quite different. Rather than randomness, temperature determines, how radically different next sample is going to be from random sampling point of view, for the current system point $s_t$.

Thus, if you specify the temperature, you can control the behaviour of the markov chain, and also how close this chain is going to resemble random samples. At high temperatures that is an independent sampling, at different temperatures the markov chain will show own behaviour.

Fin
