🧠 LLM Response:


The `logprobs` attribute in TorchServe model output when using OpenNMT-py is a list of token logarithmic probabilities of the next word. During inference or decoding time, consider the predicted sequence length as one more beyond the actual length given beforehand, and TorchServe supplies you with logarithmic probabilities of the top `k` next tokens for each position. Here `k` can be specified using attributes `token_logprobs_top_n` and `token_logprobs_top_p` in the model serving configuration, or it could default to 5 tokens for each position.

Logarithmic probabilities are used (as opposed to linear ones) to avoid encountering floating point underflow/overflow and to save precious computing memory space. For each token at each position in the predicted sequence, you'll receive `k` corresponding probabilities, which can be useful for understanding the 'neighborhood' of the predicted sequence for applications such as reranking.

To illustrate with an example, suppose we have a predicted sequence like so:

`["Python", "is", "a", "programming", "language", "[UNUSED1]", "[UNUSED2]", "[UNUSED3]", "[UNUSED4]", "[UNUSED5]"]`

(Here, `[UNUSED*]` represent the tokens with POS tag UNK in the language model.)

Then the response may contain `logprobs` similar to the following:

```python
[
    [-0.0533, -3.0007, -3.2278, -3.4870, -6.6100],
    [-1.9311, -3.1044, -3.2102, -3.6235, -6.5570],
    [-1.9641, -3.4307, -3.7125, -2.8773, -6.0304],
    [-3.4100, -3.5725, -3.7241, -3.4601, -0.1746],
    [-3.4120, -3.7234, -3.7798, -3.7449, 0.1033],
    [-5.7076, -7.0517, -7.0638, -6.5594, 0.2733],
    [-5.7115, -7.0548, -7.0684, -6.5623, 0.1753],
    [-5.7370, -7.0611, -7.0747, -6.5767, 0.2764],
    [-5.7078, -7.0520, -7.0653, -6.5631, 0.3768],
    [-5.7078, -7.0522, -7.0656, -6.5640, 0.4771]
]
```

Here the numbers are logarithms to the base e. To convert the logarithm of token into probability, you can run e^(log\_e\_probability).

In this example, the best prediction for the first position ('Next Token') is 'Python' with logarithmic probability of -0.0533 (corresponding to prob = e^(-0.0533) = 0.9481508273855965), followed by 'bash' (-3.0007), 'the' (-3.2278), 'is' (-3.4870), and 'of' (-6.6100).
[180/200] Running: How to measure and reduce TTFT on Gaudi?...

🧠 LLM Response:

Measure and reduce TTFT on Gaudi
Small scale

Gauss recurses over all ItemSelectionHandles in the InputDataHeader and performs a TTFT measurement for each one. Otherwise, VaDet hospitals recurses over all ProductionPathHandles in the InputDataHeader and performs a TTFT measurement for each one.

More specifically:

- input
	+ accumulated `total_ttft` & `successful_ttft` that were measured recursively on the parent path of the current path (if any)
	+ `passive_ttft` that was measured recursively on the parent path (if any)
- output
	+ `passive_ttft` that was measured on the current path - this is exactly `passive_ttft` of parents where traversal has been stopped with no further invocations of FollowItem deciding on producing this run
	+ `total_ttft` that is sum of passed/successful TTFT and unpassed/failed TTFT
	+ `successful_ttft` is only meaningful if there has been exact ONE successful TTFT measurement during this particular invocation
		- `unsuccessful_ttft` is the aggregated number of unsuccessful TTFT measurements

_Problems_

	+ `successful_ttft` is ambiguous - add context by checking if there is a single path handle in the <code>InputDataHeader</code> that is marked with a special flag. This will allow us distinguish between multiple successful TTFT on multiple paths (that were performed in a single purchase) and a single successful TTFT
	+ We need the information on whether this TTFT invocation finished successfully and whether this purchase was the last one
		- Success indicates that there was a single successful TTFT
		- The last purchase indicates that there will never be any more purchases

Do we measure TTFT across all cards? How to find out production speeds on different cards? Check if all cards are shown under the <code>ProductionStatus</code> panel.

Current AGDT10 does not find any TTFT data even when TTFT requests are sent. Does AGDT10 inject TTFT requests into the DaVinci DataStream?

_Afterwards_

Eventually, TTFT responses may come late (or up to not come). When a TTFT request pauses production, it should be reflected in the TTFT measurements. When a <code>TTFTUpdateRequest</code> arrives, it should reveal the information whether this request was successful and how long it has been delayed. If the request was already successful, how do we distinguish between a new TTFT on the same path and a syncing of the last TTFT request that a request was sent for? The last synchronization returns its outcome upon its arrival, while the `ActivePurchaseUpdateRequest` request is not likely to affect the current (TTFT) request that is currently in progress (e.g. `UpdateRespActivePurchase` with the "next-operation" field indicating the TTFT mechanism enabled asynchronously replaces the last `TTFTUpdateRequest` that was sent).

**Initialization**

The <code>TFC</code> is initialized by an <code>ActivePurchaseUpdateRequest</code> request to show the current accelerator online status, which is then propagated into the next request.

Ideally, the first <code>ActivePurchaseUpdateRequest</code> request should reveal the current accelerator status at the moment it is sent, either at the very beginning or long after.

_Reasons against it_

	+ There may be no accelerator available at the beginning or at least an <code>ActivePurchaseUpdateRequest</code> is not available immediately
	+ If this PROOF is repeatedly running from time to time, there might be no hot updates for a long period of time and introduce TTFT measurements only *after* there was some update. To do this,  current <code>ActivePurchaseUpdateRequest</code> which is sent by <code>TFC</code> upon its initialization is sent with a "long-request signal", that allows it to wait for LONG (e.g. hundreds of seconds) time for a hot update, while other idle <code>ActivePurchaseUpdateRequest</code> requests are being sent every minute.

At the time of the <code>ActivePurchaseUpdateRequest</code> arrival, only dimensional record with the status field initialized may be accessible which do not contain <code>Samples</code> there.
[181/200] Running: Explain the internals of HF `generate()` loop....

🧠 LLM Response:


There are two modes that influence how the HF `generate()` loop works: "train" mode and "generate" mode. Train mode corresponds to setting `model.eval()` (or alternatively running inference with debug logs off), and generation mode corresponds to setting `model.generate()`.

When generation mode is active, the first call to `generate()` triggers two entry points: `torch.script` and HF's instrumented autoregression generation (see highlighted regions in Figure 1 below), and these engender a series of nested autoregressive generation and optimization steps. Figure 1 is a high-level diagram of what exactly happens when `model.generate(n_positions)` is called, with a description underneath each highlight.

First and foremost, the call to `model.generate(n_positions)` is accompanied with the automatic pre-processing of the input prompt (if it's not already pre-processed), which could be a single token or a sequence of tokens, depending on the task.

This pre-processing usually consists of common \texttt{<bos>, <cls>, <pad>, and <eos>} special tokens being added to and altered by language-model-specific tokenizers.

Second, just before exiting the central `generate_token_ids_scripted()` function, a few important steps happen:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The notion of `token_ids` is established for future use.
    \item The \texttt{encoder} and \texttt{decoder} are run. These are standard language model building blocks that contain the bulk of the hidden state calculation logic. In this example, `model` is essentially a transformer, which implies that both encoder and decoder use the same set of hyperparameters (hidden dimensions, number of layers, attention heads, etc.). 
    \item The source input is added to the given input prompt.
    \item The eos token is calculated and added to `token_ids` after the model outputs, as is seen in the `output_ids += eos_token_ids[0]` statement just below the line marked with a red diamond.
\end{itemize}

    Generated `eos` tokens, as well as generic padding tokens, aren't used until the next generation step, when additional tokens are added recursively to the prior output. Figure 1's cyan-colored region outlines this process. Input prompts from previous iterations are stacked on one another.

    There are multiple ways to stack those prompts. In some portion of StringQA, our model's input is a concatenation of [input;footnote;previous output]. Note, there are different ways to connect prompts, for example you could connect [input; footnote] or [footnote; input], and we could attempt different combinations and see what works best for our specific use case. This paper points out a similar phenomenon exists on T5 models.\footnote{[Raffel et al., 2020](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683), Section 7.1}

    Notice that adding `previous_output` token IDs to the current prompt is done by concatenating `token_ids` with `previous_output` using `torch.cat` in the highlighted line right above the green diamond.
    The current prompt will be passed for model's handling, which includes running both \texttt{encoder} and \texttt{decoder}.
    It is possible to see how the instruction sequence incrementally grows after every succeeding iteration.

The process of initializing and extending a prompt may be repeated until the maximal number of tokens (\texttt{max_length}) that will be generated or the \texttt{eos} token is exceeded. It is important to note that length \emph{not} the number of calling iterations of \texttt{generate()} function controls the length value.

The operation fully described and demonstrated on a toy example to showcase the technical internals and the underlying concept of nesting token sequences within one another can be seen above in Appendix A. 

For each recursive call within \texttt{generate()} the top sequence(i.e prompt) incorporates enough tokens to ensure full sentential units(ups to éos token), and it will eliminate needless padding as well as consecutive duplicate tokens which otherwise may be concatenated with further iterations.

