The CATS2 decodes input bitstreams at the application level (by `cat2_decode()` call) but it seems stream decoding errors are then managed by lower level code and thrown unconditionally and unwisely back at the application.

In general, no application wants the program to crash due to decoding errors which happen, quite normally, due to incomplete data (a normal case when streams arrive as packets over the network).

One can only sandwich each `cat2_decode` call by pleading `cat2_assert_zero()` like:
```
int result = cat2_decode(c2,  inbuffer, inlength);

if (result) {
  LOG_ERROR("Error caught by cat2_decode: program will crash");
  cat2_assert_zero(result);
}
```
Yes, this branch is never taken in practice, and yet, at a certain input offset this would end up calling `goto raise_error` in file `cat2.c`, line 457 (Amlogic git commit `03707f935d330670`), which then calls `do_raise_perror()`… and this is contained in function `void raise_pedantic_error` which contains `exit(-1);`… and that is all `exit` and macro `assert` do in any C program that I know of.

So is there a graceful way to deal with decoding errors without having the program terminate abruptly? Also, is there a modern and updated version of this code?
[185/200] Running: How to enable/disable kv caching in transformers?...

🧠 LLM Response:

-------------------------------------------------

To enable/disable caching of model keys for transformers (e.g. ``model_module``), set
the `transformers_kv_caching` attribute of the `experimental_get_adversarial_params` context to True/False in
`attacks.adversarial_params_generator()`.
```python
from numpy.random import RandomState
import torch
import adversarial_robustness_toolbox as art

# Generates a PGD adversarial attack
pgd = art.attacks.DeepFool(classifier,
                           loss_fct='soft_max',
                           num_classes=10,
                           targeted=True)

# Create input.
x = torch.randn(1, 784)
x = x.cuda()

classifier.set_train(False)

# Set the context attribute transformers_kv_caching
art.classifiers.set_default_context_settings(transfromers_kv_caching=False)

# Run the attack on an instance.
PGD_perturbed_instance = pgd.perturb(x)

# How to get adversarial input with model keys caching enabled and how many keys have been stored?
art.classifiers.set_default_context_settings(transfromers_kv_caching=True)
print("Keys get stored:", pgd.keys_stored)

PGD_perturbed_instance_2 = pgd.perturb(x)

# number of keys cached.
print("# keys cached:", pgd.parser.keys_cached)
```
When `transformers_kv_caching` is set to True, adversarial augmentation with keys is done,
but the computation required to get those keys is skipped and instead keys are loaded from cache.

When `transformers_kv_caching` is set to False, adversarial augmentation is computationally intensive.


When caching is enabled, the number of keys that can be cached is limited by the available GPU memory. Once the memory
limits are reached, adversarial augmentation with keys may take long time to run.


