[186/200] Running: What are challenges of multilingual streaming inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 What are the main pitfalls of current methods?

Multilingual streaming inference is the process of producing real-time translations of live or near-live text data by simultaneously dealing with data in multiple languages. Compared to conventional multilingual non-streaming methods, this area of research has only recently gained broader attention. Multilingual streaming inference has multiple exciting applications, including online forums, chats, live conversations, and other areas where data becomes available in real-time.

One of the main challenges of multilingual streaming inference is that algorithms have to perform real-time translation under severe time constraints. Non-streaming multilingual methods can optimize for translation quality by using linguistic resources and pre-trained models, which are often computationally demanding and time-consuming. On the other hand, streaming methods have to process data without bottlenecks and ensure that no single packet takes longer than the expected delay in transmission. This is particularly challenging when dealing with multiple languages, where the translations can significantly differ from each other in speed and the amount of processing required.

Another challenge of multilingual streaming inference is the need to deal with highly imbalanced sequences. Traditional multilingual models can easily adjust to specific characteristics of a language by using targeted embedding spaces or designing unique models for a specific language. Streaming algorithms, on the other hand, have to deal with time-varying delays, packet loss, and languages of varying content-length that can appear in random order. Therefore, multilingual streaming, designed as a single monolithic architecture, can significantly underperform compared to tailored monolingual solutions. However, deploying a separate model for each language also increases the complexity and overhead of the algorithm, making the design of an adaptive streaming architecture crucial for ensuring real-time operation.

Lastly, another common challenge that faces multilingual streaming inference is integrating real-time speech-to-text with translation workflows. While there are several state-of-the-art commercial solutions for transcribing speech to text, most of them are designed for single-language transcriptions and generate low-quality results when used for multilingual use-cases. Consequently, numerous pitfalls arise from integrating these technologies into the streaming pipeline, including increased processing times, low-quality transcriptions, and rigid design choices that limit the applicability of the algorithm.

In conclusion, multilingual streaming inference faces numerous challenges, including time constraints, language imbalances, and integration with other technologies. Many of these challenges arise due to the real-time nature of the task, which complicates the design of the system compared to non-streaming counterparts. As a result, current methods often suffer from specific pitfalls related to these challenges, such as poor translation quality, high processing times, and rigidity in design choices. Consequently, research in this area remains crucial for overcoming these limitations and improving multilingual streaming inference algorithms.
